        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          There is no fuel-burning generator you can safely run indoors, whatever the label says about CO sensors or propane. The only indoor generator is a battery power station. Pick it by the job: a light unit for the bedroom, a kilowatt-hour for the fridge, or a 240V unit on a transfer switch for the house.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="never-indoors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why No Fuel-Burning Generator Is Safe Indoors
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Fuel-Burning Generator Qualifies</h3>

          <p>
            Start with the rule that makes this whole page necessary: there is no such thing as a gas, propane, diesel or dual fuel generator that is safe to run inside a house. Not in a basement, not in an attached garage with the door up, not in a crawlspace, not on a screened porch. The CDC's guidance is blunt about it: never use a generator inside a home, basement, garage or crawlspace, even with windows and doors open, and keep it at least 20 feet from any window, door or vent. The CPSC says the same, and the numbers behind the warning are why both agencies keep repeating it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Carbon Monoxide Numbers</h3>

          <p>
            The CPSC estimates an average of about 85 people a year in the United States died from carbon monoxide poisoning tied to portable generators over 2017 to 2019, and its multi-year report counts 765 non-fire CO deaths associated with portable generators between 2009 and 2019. The agency's earlier reviews found that most of those deaths happened with the generator in a basement, a garage or the living space itself. The CPSC has warned that a single portable generator can put out as much carbon monoxide as hundreds of modern cars, and in an enclosed space the level climbs in minutes, not hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Two Features That Do Not Make It Safe</h3>

          <p>
            Two features get misread as making indoor use acceptable, and neither does. The first is a CO shutoff sensor, sold under names like CO Shield, CO-MINDER and COsense. These are a genuine safety improvement, and we favor generators that have them, but they shut the engine off after carbon monoxide has already built up around the unit. They are a last line of defense for a generator placed outdoors in the wrong spot, not permission to bring it in. The second is propane. Propane burns cleaner than gasoline in terms of soot and smell, but it still produces carbon monoxide, and a propane generator in a garage is as dangerous as a gasoline one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Only Thing That Qualifies</h3>

          <p>
            So when people search for an indoor generator, the honest answer is that the only thing that qualifies is a battery power station: a lithium battery, an inverter and a charger in one case, with no engine and no exhaust. That is what every pick on this page is. If you also own a gas generator, it belongs outdoors at the distances in our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a>, and the power station is what runs inside the house. Our explainer on <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/">whether power stations are safe indoors</a> covers why the battery side of that arrangement is safe; this page is about which one to buy for which room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Engines go outside. Batteries go inside.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Any generator that burns fuel produces carbon monoxide, and CO sensors, propane and open doors do not change that. If it has to run inside the house, it has to be a battery.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What an Indoor Generator Actually Is
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storage, Not Generation</h3>

          <p>
            A battery power station does not generate anything. It stores energy you put into it earlier, from a wall outlet, a solar panel, a car socket or a gas generator running outside, and releases it through ordinary household outlets. Calling it a generator is marketing shorthand that stuck, and it is worth keeping in mind because it explains the single biggest difference from the real thing: a gas generator runs for as long as you keep pouring fuel into it, while a power station runs until its battery is empty and then needs a recharge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watt-Hours and Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Two numbers describe every unit. Watt-hours (Wh) are the size of the tank: a 1,024Wh unit can deliver roughly 1,024 watts for an hour, or 100 watts for about ten hours, minus a 10 to 15 percent inverter loss. Watts (W) are how much it can deliver at once: an 1,800W unit can run anything up to 1,800W continuously, and a surge rating covers the brief startup kick a compressor or pump needs. Buyers who get this wrong usually buy on output and run out of capacity, because a high-output unit will happily drain itself in half an hour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Features That Matter Indoors</h3>

          <p>
            Indoors, three features matter more than they would at a campsite. Switchover time, the gap between grid power dropping and the battery taking over, decides whether a CPAP, a desktop computer or a router keeps running or restarts; under 20 milliseconds is the usual target, and under 10 is better. Fan noise matters because the unit sits in a room you are in. And battery chemistry matters because the unit lives in your house all year, charging and sitting at full charge, which is where lithium iron phosphate earns its place.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Cannot Do</h3>

          <p>
            What a power station cannot do is replace a gas generator for long outages without a way to refill it. A 1kWh unit running a refrigerator will last roughly a day, depending on the fridge, and a CPAP for several nights. Past that you need solar, a car, or a small generator outside to recharge it. Our <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">power station vs generator comparison</a> goes through that trade-off in full; here, the point is to size the battery to the room and the job so the recharge question comes up as rarely as possible.
          </p>
        </div>

