          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bedroom and CPAP</h3>

          <p>
            Bedroom and CPAP use is the most common indoor job and the one where small mistakes hurt most. A CPAP without heated humidification usually draws 30 to 60 watts, so a kilowatt-hour unit covers several nights, but switching on the heated humidifier can double or triple that. If you can, run the machine from its DC input with the manufacturer's DC cord, which skips the inverter loss entirely. Our guide on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/">a power station can run a CPAP</a> has the runtime maths by machine type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Refrigerator</h3>

          <p>
            Refrigerator use is about surge and duty cycle. The compressor runs perhaps a third to a half of the time and draws a brief startup spike several times its running watts each time it kicks in. Any of the 1,500W-plus units here start a normal full-size fridge; the question is capacity, and a 1kWh unit typically gives you roughly a day, depending on the model and how often the door opens. The kitchen is also where a high continuous rating pays off, because someone will plug in a coffee maker. Our page on <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">running a refrigerator from a power station</a> covers how to measure your own fridge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-Home Backup</h3>

          <p>
            Whole-home backup is a different category. Running a well pump, a furnace blower or several circuits needs 240V output, a transfer switch or inlet, and several kilowatt-hours, which today means a unit like the DELTA Pro 3 or a stack of batteries. It also costs as much as a good gas generator and an installed transfer switch combined. If whole-home coverage is the real goal, read our guide to <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">power stations for home backup</a> alongside this one, because it compares the large units on the things that matter at that scale.
          </p>
