        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. UL and ETL Listings: What to Check Before It Lives in Your House
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Listings Matter Indoors</h3>

          <p>
            A power station sits in your home charging for months at a time, often unattended, often overnight. That is a different exposure from a camping trip, and it is the strongest argument for buying a unit with a recognised safety listing. In the US the relevant standard for portable power stations is UL 2743, which covers portable power packs, and the mark you look for is either UL's own or ETL, Intertek's mark, which tests products to the same UL standards. Both are Nationally Recognized Testing Laboratories, and both are acceptable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Listing Means</h3>

          <p>
            What the listing means in practice is that the unit's battery management, charging circuit, enclosure and outlets have been tested for overcharge, short circuit, overheating and abuse, and that the factory is audited to keep building it the same way. It does not mean the unit cannot fail. It does mean somebody independent has checked the failure modes that cause fires. An FCC logo, by contrast, is about radio interference and says nothing about fire safety, and a CE mark on a US-market product is a self-declaration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Verify It in the Certifier's Directory</h3>

          <p>
            Do not take a listing on trust from a product page. Search the certifier's own directory, UL Product iQ for UL and Intertek's Directory of Building Products for ETL, using the manufacturer name and model number. Retailer listings for the larger brands generally cite UL certification, but marketing copy sometimes describes a component, such as the cells, as certified when the finished unit is not, or cites a standard without a listing. The model number check takes two minutes and is the most useful thing you can do before buying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UL 9540 for Wired-In Units</h3>

          <p>
            For a unit wired to the house through a transfer switch or inlet, local inspectors may also look for an energy storage listing such as UL 9540, which covers stationary storage systems. That is another reason to ask the electrician installing the switch which listings your jurisdiction expects before you buy a large unit. And whatever you buy, avoid unbranded units sold under rotating names with no listing at all; a no-name 1kWh pack that is a third cheaper is exactly the product a safety listing exists to screen out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifepo4" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. LiFePO4: The Chemistry to Insist On Indoors
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Every Pick Is LFP</h3>

          <p>
            Every pick on this page uses lithium iron phosphate (LiFePO4, often written LFP) cells, and that is deliberate. Older and cheaper power stations used nickel manganese cobalt (NMC) cells, the chemistry in most laptops and many electric cars. NMC packs are lighter for the same capacity, but they tolerate heat and abuse less well and wear out faster. For a unit that sits in a bedroom or a utility room for years, LFP is the right trade.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thermal Stability</h3>

          <p>
            The safety difference comes down to thermal stability. LFP cells have a much higher thermal runaway threshold than NMC and release far less energy if they do fail, which is why the chemistry is used in home battery systems that live in garages and basements. No lithium battery is risk-free, and a crushed or swollen pack of any chemistry should go outside and stay there, but LFP is the chemistry that makes indoor storage an ordinary decision rather than a calculated one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lifespan</h3>

          <p>
            The second benefit is lifespan. The manufacturers of the units here rate their LFP packs for about 4,000 cycles to 80 percent of original capacity, against the 500 to 800 cycles commonly quoted for NMC. For an indoor backup that might see a few dozen deep cycles a year, that is the difference between a unit that lasts the decade and one that is noticeably weaker after a few years. LFP also handles being kept at a high state of charge better, which is how a backup unit spends most of its life.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Trade-Off: Weight</h3>

          <p>
            The cost is weight. LFP stores less energy per pound, which is why a 1kWh LFP unit weighs 24 to 35 pounds where an older NMC unit of the same size could be closer to 20. Indoors, that rarely matters: the unit lives on the floor near the fridge or by the bed. Our full comparison of <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 and lithium-ion power stations</a> goes into cycle counts and real-world ageing in more detail, but the short version for indoor use is simple. If a unit is still NMC in 2026, skip it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fan-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fan Noise: The Only Sound an Indoor Generator Makes
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Noise Comes From</h3>

          <p>
            A power station has no engine, but it is not silent. The inverter and the charger both produce heat, and once the internal temperature rises, cooling fans spin up. Under a light load like a CPAP, a phone and a lamp, most current units stay quiet or silent. Under a heavy load, such as a fridge compressor starting or a coffee maker running, and especially while fast charging, the fans can be clearly audible in a quiet bedroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reading Noise Ratings</h3>

          <p>
            Manufacturers publish noise ratings inconsistently, so read them carefully. EcoFlow rates the DELTA 3 Plus under 30 dB at loads below 600W and under 40 dB below 1,200W, and rates the RIVER 3 under 30 dB. For the DELTA Pro 3, EcoFlow quotes about 30 dB at light loads, and one retailer spec sheet lists 35 dB under 2,000W at 25 degrees Celsius. Jackery and BLUETTI publish no decibel figures for the Explorer 1000 v2 and the AC180, so for those we rely on owner reports, which commonly describe the fans as quiet at bedside loads and noticeable during fast charging. For scale, 30 dB is roughly a whisper, and a typical kitchen refrigerator hums somewhere around 40.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Charging Speed Is the Biggest Lever</h3>

          <p>
            The biggest noise lever is when you charge. The fastest charging modes, like the Explorer 1000 v2's one-hour app mode or the SOLIX C1000 Gen 2's 49-minute recharge, are when fans run hardest. Charge during the day, or switch to a slower or quiet charging mode overnight; most of these units offer one in the app. The second lever is load. A heated humidifier, an electric blanket or a space heater on a power station will both drain the battery quickly and keep the fans running, which is two good reasons not to do it in a bedroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Placement</h3>

          <p>
            Placement helps too. Give the unit a few inches of clearance around its vents so the fans do not have to work as hard, keep it off thick carpet and bedding that block the intake, and keep it out of a closed cabinet, where heat builds and the fans run constantly. A hard floor or a small table a few feet from the bed is the usual answer. If silence matters more than anything else, a bigger battery running at a small fraction of its rating will be quieter than a small one working near its limit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transfer-switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Feeding House Circuits: Inlets and Transfer Switches
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plug In First</h3>

          <p>
            Most indoor users will plug appliances straight into the power station and leave it there, and for a fridge, a CPAP and some lights that is the right approach. Once you want to run hard-wired loads, such as a furnace blower, a well pump or the kitchen circuit, you need a proper connection to the house wiring, and the rules are the same as for a gas generator: a transfer switch or an interlock, installed by an electrician, so the power station can never send power back out onto the utility lines.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Use a Double-Ended Cord</h3>

          <p>
            Never use a double-ended cord, sometimes called a suicide cord, to backfeed a wall outlet. It energises the outlet's exposed prongs, it can push power into the grid and injure a line worker, and when the utility power returns it can destroy the unit or start a fire. The fact that a battery has no exhaust does not make backfeeding any safer. This is the most dangerous shortcut in home backup and it is no less dangerous with a battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-Home Units: Inlet or Transfer Switch</h3>

          <p>
            For a whole-home unit like the DELTA Pro 3, the connection is usually its NEMA L14-30 output to a power inlet box on the outside wall or a manual transfer switch beside the panel, exactly as with a 30-amp portable generator. That route also lets a 240V unit run a 240V well pump. Our guides to <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/">generator inlet boxes</a>, <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">manual transfer switches</a> and <a href="/how-to-hook-up-a-generator-to-your-house/">how to hook a generator up to your house</a> cover the hardware and what the installation involves. Some brands also sell their own home panels that integrate the battery with selected circuits; those cost more but switch automatically.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">120V Units and Small Transfer Switches</h3>

          <p>
            A 120V-only unit like the DELTA 3 Plus or the SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 can still feed a small manual transfer switch that serves 120V circuits, and some owners do exactly that to run a gas furnace blower and the refrigerator circuit without cords through the house. The limit is capacity, not wiring: 1kWh runs out fast once a furnace blower is involved. If that is the plan, our explainer on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-gas-furnace/">a power station can run a gas furnace</a> has the numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recharging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Recharging During a Long Outage: Where Gas Comes Back In
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Long the Battery Lasts</h3>

          <p>
            A power station only solves the indoor problem for as long as its battery lasts. For an outage of a few hours, or a night, any of the mid-size picks will carry a fridge and a bedroom without a recharge. For a multi-day storm outage, you need a way to put energy back in, and there are only three: sunlight, a vehicle or a generator outdoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar: Clean but Slow</h3>

          <p>
            Solar is the cleanest answer and the slowest. A 200W panel in good sun might put 100 to 160 watts into a battery, which over a few productive hours is enough to replace what a CPAP uses overnight but not what a refrigerator uses in a day. A 12V car socket is slower still, typically around 100 watts, and means running the car outdoors, never in an attached garage. Both are worth having as a backup and neither is a full replacement for the grid.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Generator Outside, Power Station Inside</h3>

          <p>
            The most practical long-outage setup is a small inverter generator running outdoors, well away from the house, feeding the power station's AC input through an outdoor-rated extension cord. The generator runs for a couple of hours in the daytime, the station charges at full speed, and then the generator goes off and the house runs silently from the battery overnight. A 1,000 to 1,500 watt inverter is enough to charge most of these units at or near full speed; our guide to the <a href="/best-1500-watt-generator/">best 1500 watt generators</a> covers the class, and it is the cheapest way to turn a one-day battery into a one-week system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Two Rules Separate</h3>

          <p>
            If you do this, keep the two rules separate in your head. The generator obeys every generator rule: outdoors, 20 feet from windows and doors, exhaust pointing away, a CO alarm in the house. The power station obeys the battery rules: inside, dry, ventilated, not in a sealed cabinet. The cord between them is the only thing that crosses the threshold. For a broader view of outage planning, our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">portable generator guide</a> covers the gas side in full.
          </p>
        </div>

