          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there any generator that is safe to use indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No fuel-burning generator is safe indoors, including propane and inverter models and those with CO shutoff sensors. The CDC and CPSC both say never run one inside a home, basement, garage or crawlspace. Battery power stations, which have no engine and no exhaust, are the only units designed for indoor use, and they are what people usually mean by an indoor generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a propane generator inside my house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Propane burns cleaner than gasoline in terms of smell and soot, but it still produces carbon monoxide, and CO is what kills. A propane generator in a garage or basement is as dangerous as a gasoline one. Indoor propane heaters rated for indoor use are a separate category with oxygen depletion sensors; generators are not.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size indoor generator do I need for a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for at least 1,000Wh and 1,500W of continuous output. A typical full-size refrigerator averages 100 to 200 watts over a day with brief startup surges, so a 1kWh unit usually runs it for roughly a day. The Anker SOLIX C1000 Gen 2 and EcoFlow DELTA 3 Plus are both good fits; check your own fridge's label.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a battery generator run a CPAP all night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, easily, if you size it right. A CPAP without heated humidification typically draws 30 to 60 watts, so a 1kWh unit like the Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 covers several nights. A 245Wh unit like the EcoFlow RIVER 3 manages about one. Heated humidifiers can triple the draw, so turn the heat off during an outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are indoor power stations loud?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Much quieter than any engine. They make no sound at idle and only run cooling fans under heavier loads or while fast charging. EcoFlow rates the DELTA 3 Plus under 30 dB at loads below 600W. To keep a bedroom quiet, charge during the day and keep the load light overnight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an indoor generator power my whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a large unit, and only selected circuits. The EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3 offers 4,000W at 120V or 240V and an L14-30 output for a transfer switch, and expands to about 12kWh. That runs essentials like a fridge, well pump and furnace blower, but not central air or an electric range for long.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put a power station indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a hard, dry surface with a few inches of clearance around the vents, away from water, direct sun and heat sources. Avoid closed cabinets and thick carpet that block airflow. Keep it where you can see it while it charges, and if it is ever swollen, damaged or smells odd, move it outside and stop using it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a gas generator in the garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An open garage door does not clear carbon monoxide, and an attached garage shares air with the house. The CDC and CPSC both say never in a garage, basement or crawlspace, even with doors open. A CO shutoff sensor is a backstop for an outdoor generator placed badly, not a licence to bring one in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on watts and running out of watt-hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2,000W rating says what the unit can run at once, not for how long. On 1,024Wh, 2,000W lasts about half an hour. Size the battery to the overnight or all-day job first, then make sure the output covers the largest load and its startup surge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a heated humidifier on a small battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A CPAP's heated humidifier and heated tube can double or triple its draw. On a 245Wh unit that turns one night into a few hours. Turn the heat off during an outage, use the DC cord where the maker sells one, and size up if you cannot sleep without it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the certification check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Search the UL or ETL directory for the exact model number before buying. Product pages sometimes describe certified cells or a standard rather than a listed unit. For something that charges overnight in your house for years, two minutes in a certifier's database is cheap insurance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding an outlet with a double-ended cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A battery has no exhaust, but a suicide cord is just as dangerous on one: live exposed prongs, power pushed toward line workers, and damage when the grid returns. House circuits need an inlet and transfer switch or interlock, installed by an electrician.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

